            <div xml:id="d477">

                <pb facs="0488" n="412" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg412"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 865.111/15</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Jay to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1916</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>634. Referring to the Embassy’s number 633, May 4. Permanent Undersecretary of
                    State informed me this evening that matter was receiving very sympathetic
                    consideration of the Foreign Office and other departments and that he felt
                    certain that some arrangement will be effected within the next few days by which
                    in practice, even if not officially, wives and children will be permitted to
                    leave. I received distinct impression Foreign Office most anxious to arrange
                    this matter so as to avoid any ill feeling or irritation in the United
                    States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jay</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
